\section*{An irreducible tensor square containing every symmetric-group representation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For each symmetric-group degree other than $2,4,9$, the authors claim an irreducible representation whose square contains every irreducible constituent.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For every positive integer \(n\notin\{2,4,9\}\), the source claims a partition \(\lambda\vdash n\) satisfying
\[
 \forall\nu\vdash n,\qquad g(\lambda,\lambda,\nu)>0.
\]
Thus one irreducible complex representation of \(S_n\) has every irreducible as a constituent of its tensor square. The three exceptional sizes are excluded from this theorem.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The construction selects a self-conjugate partition in each permitted degree and extends the cyclic polytabloid method beyond staircases. Combinatorial decomposition and exact capacity checks cover the remaining degrees; positivity transfers to corresponding unipotent tensor-square multiplicities by the cited character-theoretic comparison.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The universal tensor-square theorem supplies a partition lambda for each positive n outside \{2,4,9\}, with g(lambda,lambda,nu)>0 for every nu. lean/docs/205.md separately documents this exact universal result. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 205, the Lean scope includes every staircase tensor square and the universal irreducible squares away from degrees $2,4,9$; occurrence inside the orbit of the prescribed cyclic vector is extra manuscript scope. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{Saxl.lean}, \texttt{UniversalTensorSquares.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Saxl.saxl\_conjecture}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

